  "verdict": "The largest movement in hit@10 across the 8 finished pair(s) is +0.0500 in `dense` / `contextual_prefix` (paired McNemar p = 0.5000). Atomicity itself did happen: 87% of the recursive chunks that exceed the budget carry a table row or a code fence, so the splitter kept them whole at the cost of the token budget, and the share of chunks ending mid-sentence falls from 0.5745 to 0.1865. The one thing chunk strategy does move is how often an answer carries a supported citation: 1 of 8 pairs shift significantly, the largest being +7 items in `dense/contextual_prefix` (p = 0.0156), and the sign is not the same in every pair \u2014 so the mechanism buys citation coverage rather than retrieval quality. The `table_content` items \u2014 the golden questions whose answer sits in a table \u2014 do not move significantly in any pair, so keeping tables atomic did not measurably help the questions it was meant to help. On this corpus the atomic-table hypothesis does not survive: chunk strategy changes how chunks are built and how often an answer is cited, not how well retrieval works."
